Hi Lauri,

Did the evtest test. Scrolling in a terminal always feels fine, but in 
browser I can clearly feel it "scrolling too much", so I did it there.

Right after receiver connect, no Solaar running, every REL_WHEEL_HI_RES 
event has value 15 - never 120. So the value itself looks correct. But 
when I counted how many of these fire between each legacy REL_WHEEL 
click, most are 8 (correct), but quite a few are 9, 10, even 11. So 
instead of 120 units per physical click it's sometimes up to 165 - 
that's the "scrolls too much" I'm seeing, it's not a wrong value, it's 
extra events.

evtest log attached

Frantisek

Dne 04. 10. 26 v 16:31 Lauri Saurus napsal(a):
> Hi.
>
> Adding the regressions list back to the discussion, as I accidentally omitted it from the recipients of my previous reply.
>
> The debug log patch and log file were not sent to the list, so I’m attaching them here for reference.
>
>> Thanks again for digging into this. Built and loaded your debug patch on top of f0866517be93 (Fedora 44 kernel-7.2.8 sources), rebuilt into the initramfs so it's active from early boot. Log attached (wheeldbg.log).
>>
>> Result across every single transition logged (9 total, covering both driver-initiated SetWheelMode and ones triggered externally via Solaar): SetWheelMode's answer and the follow-up GetWheelMode always
>> *agree* -- wheel_multiplier and the device's self-reported mode never disagree once. Yet the actual scroll behavior stays broken/undivided the whole time regardless of which mode is active or agreed-upon.
>>
>> So in my case it doesn't look like a SetWheelMode/GetWheelMode cache-desync at all -- both sides consistently agree, but the visible scroll output doesn't follow what either side thinks the mode is. That
>> suggests the actual REL_WHEEL_HI_RES scaling happening on the wire/in the input layer isn't being driven by wheel_multiplier the way we'd expect, or something downstream of this driver is involved.
> Based on the debug log you sent, I can't see the reason why this commit would break the hires scroll on G903. Especially if it is broken right away after plugging the receiver, without any user space intervention. After receiver connect, I only see logs "SetWheelMode answer (kernel request)". These are just debug logging but there is nothing there that the commit f0866517be9345d8245d32b722574b8aecccb348 changed. "SetWheelMode answer" logs show the place where the commit actually changes existing functionality by modifying the multiplier which before the commit was statically always 8 if hires capability was found from mouse and 1 if not. Logs show that the SetWheelMode responses come as expected and multiplier is updated as expected.
>
> What is "sudo evtest" showing right after receiver connect when you scroll? With Solaar not running.
